{:concept/id :framework/protocol-extension-boundary
 :concept/type :framework
 :concept/layer :framework-explanation
 :concept/name "Framework–protocol extension ownership boundary"
 :concept/summary
 "The framework–protocol extension ownership boundary distinguishes responsibilities between the generic protocol-independent framework, protocol-specific extensions (adapters), and concrete accounting layers. It records where abstract policies and artifacts live, where protocol-state translation happens, and where concrete ledger mutation occurs, so that the generic framework is not conflated with a protocol-specific implementation."

 :concept/stakeholder-question
 "Which framework layer owns abstract policies and verification, which protocol extension owns protocol-state translation, and which layer owns concrete ledger mutation?"

 :concept/protocols #{:protocol/sew-v1 :protocol/prf}

 :concept/related
[:framework/held-custody
   :framework/research-command
   :framework/review-certificate
   :concept/conservation
   :security/force-authorisation]

 :concept/note
  "Ownership boundaries:
   - The generic framework owns abstract policies, artifacts, and verification.
   - Protocol-specific extensions (adapters) own translation between protocol state and generic inputs/effects.
   - Concrete accounting owns protocol-ledger mutation.
   - Receipts bind abstract effects to concrete state changes.
   A source-code import prohibition (e.g. the generic framework must not import a specific protocol extension) is an architectural direction; it does not by itself prove the framework is protocol-independent."

 :concept/roles
 {:framework-owner {:description "Entity or layer responsible for generic policies, artifacts, and verification."
                    :maps-to [:protocol.actor/resolution-module]}
  :extension-owner {:description "Protocol extension layer responsible for translating protocol state to/from generic inputs and effects."
                  :maps-to [:protocol.actor/resolution-module]}
  :accounting-owner {:description "Layer responsible for concrete protocol-ledger mutation."
                     :maps-to [:protocol.actor/resolution-module]}}

 :concept/entities
 {:generic-framework {:description "Protocol-independent layer owning abstract policies, artifacts, and verification."
                      :maps-to [:protocol.entity/module]}
  :protocol-extension {:description "Protocol extension layer translating protocol-specific state to/from generic inputs and effects."
                     :maps-to [:protocol.entity/module]}
  :concrete-accounting {:description "Layer owning concrete protocol-ledger mutation."
                        :maps-to [:protocol.entity/module]}
  :binding-receipt {:description "Artifact binding an abstract effect to a concrete state change."
                    :maps-to [:protocol.entity/evidence]}}

 :concept/actions
 {:enforce-boundary {:description "Enforce the import/ownership boundary between layers."
                     :maps-to [:protocol.action/check-invariant]}
  :verify-ownership {:description "Verify that each responsibility is held by the correct layer."
                     :maps-to [:protocol.action/verify-allocation]}
  :translate-state {:description "Translate protocol state to/from generic inputs and effects via an adapter."
                    :maps-to [:protocol.action/parameterise]}}

 :concept/outcomes
 {:boundary-respected {:description "Responsibilities are held by the intended layers."
                       :maps-to [:protocol.outcome/released]}
  :boundary-violated {:description "A layer took on a responsibility outside its ownership."
                      :maps-to [:protocol.outcome/adversarial-resolution]}}

 :concept/failure-modes
 [{:failure/id :framework-protocol-extension-coupling
   :failure/name "Framework–protocol extension coupling"
   :failure/summary "The generic framework depends on a protocol-specific extension, breaking protocol independence."
   :protocol-mechanisms [:import-boundary :adapter]
   :stakeholder-impact "The generic framework can no longer serve other protocols independently."}]

 :concept/assumptions
 ["The generic framework owns abstract policies, artifacts, and verification."
  "Protocol-specific extensions (adapters) own translation between protocol state and generic inputs/effects."
  "Concrete accounting owns protocol-ledger mutation."
  "Receipts bind abstract effects to concrete state changes."
  "An import prohibition is an architectural direction, not proof that the framework is protocol-independent by itself."
  "Concept labels do not change protocol validity."]

 :concept/out-of-scope
 ["Runtime process isolation."
  "Permission or authorisation enforcement."
  "Deployment-level sandboxing."
  "Absence of semantic coupling through shared data structures."
  "Dependency integrity or package pinning."
  "Protocol-specific behaviour implemented in adapters."
  "External services called dynamically or through configuration."
  "Scope-authorised concrete-accounting ledger mutation — covered by the :force-authorisation/scope-authorisation mandate (see security/force_authorisation.edn): the low-level ledger/index write runs only after the applicable scope (position/account partitioning, and force-authorisation status/scope for exceptional reasons) has been validated. The low-level write is an internal implementation detail, not an independent public mutation surface."]

 :concept/metrics
 {:adapter-count {:description "Number of protocol extensions (adapters) bound to the generic framework."
                  :source :derived-from-trace}}}
